// The Utah Teapot's geometry -- Martin Newell, 1975 -- as a primitive, `Teapot()`, for any scene to
// include.  `the-utah-teapot.igl` tells its story and stands it on a counter in brass.
//
// **The thirty-two bicubic patches below are the classic dataset**, transcribed verbatim from POV-Ray's
// `teapot.inc` (the Newell/Blinn/Enzmann data, adapted for POV-Ray by Alexander Enzmann), which is
// licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 3.0: http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/
//
// The numbers are unaltered.  Newell's data stands up in Z, so `rotate X -90` turns it rather than the
// coordinates being rewritten.  See `the-utah-teapot.igl` for why each patch is diced to `flatness 0.0001`.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The dataset.  Thirty-two patches: sixteen for the body, four for the lid, four for the handle,
// four for the spout, and four underneath.  See the note at the top of this file for its source
// and license.  The four lid patches and the four bottom ones each collapse an entire row of
// control points onto a single point -- the apex and the center of the base -- which is a pole, and
// perfectly legal.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

